TITLE:: MumpsPort
summary:: A port of legacy MUMPS database logic for auditory scene analysis.
categories:: Database, Analysis
related:: Classes/ASAManager, Classes/F0TrackerDB

DESCRIPTION::
MumpsPort is a collection of classes that port the logic originally implemented in MUMPS (Massachusetts General Hospital Utility Multi-Programming System) for the Auditory Scene Analysis (ASA) tracker. 

MUMPS was a key technology in 1960s and 70s medical informatics and early AI, known for its hierarchical tree-structured database and string manipulation capabilities. This port brings that "old school" architectural philosophy into SuperCollider, providing a structured way to manage the complex, hierarchical data generated by multi-pitch tracking.

The port includes:
LIST::
## STRONG::ASAManager::: Port of ASA.m. Handles global session management and "Auditory Object" formation.
## STRONG::F0TrackerDB::: Port of F0TRACK.m. A database-like interface for storing and retrieving pitch observations.
## STRONG::PerceptualModel::: Port of PERCEPT.m. Implements Bayesian updates and leaky integrators for perceptual persistence.
## STRONG::TrackingLogger::: Port of LOGGER.m. Provides structured event logging.
## STRONG::ScaleUtils::: Port of SCALES.m. Utility functions for frequency conversions (Cents, ERB, MIDI).
::

CLASSMETHODS::

INSTANCEMETHODS::

EXAMPLES::

code::
// Using the F0TrackerDB
d = F0TrackerDB.new;
d.pushFrame(0, 0, 440, 0.9);
d.pushFrame(0, 1, 660, 0.8);
d.processFrames;
d.report;

// Using ScaleUtils
ScaleUtils.freqToErb(1000);
ScaleUtils.cents(440, 442);
::

THEORETICAL BACKGROUND::
The decision to port MUMPS logic to SuperCollider reflects a commitment to "technological archeology." MUMPS's approach to sparse, multi-dimensional arrays is surprisingly well-suited to the sparse nature of auditory events. In this system, we treat the auditory scene as a hierarchical database where "sessions" contain "objects" which contain "tracks" which contain "observations."